// Longer explanations for the catalogue (js/lib/phenomena.js), keyed by entry name:
// [what is going on and why, a worked example with real numbers]. HTML with \( \) math.
const r = String.raw;

export const DETAILS = {
  // ── Geometrical image formation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Refraction (Snell's law)": [r`Snell's law follows from continuity of the tangential wave vector at the boundary, or equivalently from Fermat's principle. Every lens surface is a place where it acts, and its non-linearity in angle (sin rather than angle) is the root of all monochromatic aberrations: the paraxial approximation replaces \(\sin\theta\) by \(\theta\).`, r`Light entering crown glass (n = 1.52) at 45° continues at 27.7°. The paraxial estimate 45°/1.52 = 29.6° is already 2° off.`],
  "Thin-lens imaging": [r`For a lens whose thickness is negligible, the conjugate distances obey the Gaussian lens equation and the lateral magnification is their ratio. Real lenses obey the same law if distances are measured from the principal planes.`, r`A 50 mm lens focused at 2 m forms the image 51.3 mm behind it, at magnification −0.026: a 1.8 m person is 46 mm tall on the sensor.`],
  "Thick lenses & cardinal points": [r`Any rotationally symmetric system, however many elements, acts paraxially like a thin lens located at two principal planes, with nodal points that coincide with them in air. The ray-transfer (ABCD) matrix of the system gives all of them.`, r`A telephoto with 300 mm focal length can be 200 mm long: its rear principal plane lies in front of the first element.`],
  "Entrance & exit pupils": [r`The aperture stop limits the cone of light; the pupils are its images through the optics in front of and behind it. The entrance pupil is the centre of perspective and sets the f-number; the exit pupil sets the angle of the chief rays at the sensor.`, r`In a wide-angle retrofocus lens the entrance pupil looks larger off-axis and sits near the front, while the exit pupil is far behind the lens.`],
  "No-parallax point (panoramas)": [r`Near and far objects keep their alignment only if the camera rotates about the entrance pupil, the point all chief rays aim at. The nodal point is the wrong pivot for a rotating camera: it matters for rotating the lens alone in a panoramic camera.`, r`With the pupil 8 cm in front of the pivot and 30° between frames, an object 1 m away shifts by about 2.4° relative to the background: an obvious ghost.`],
  "f-number & numerical aperture": [r`The f-number is the focal length over the entrance-pupil diameter; for an aplanatic lens at infinity it equals \(1/(2\,\mathrm{NA}')\) exactly. Irradiance scales as \(1/N^2\), so each full stop (factor \(\sqrt2\)) halves the light.`, r`An f/1.4 lens collects 16 times more light per unit image area than f/5.6, and its image-side cone has a half-angle of 21°.`],
  "Étendue & brightness theorem": [r`Étendue measures the phase-space volume of a beam; lossless optics conserve it and no passive system can reduce it. Consequently the radiance of an image cannot exceed that of the source (divided by n²): optics can concentrate power only by giving up angle.`, r`The Sun imaged by a burning glass can heat a target at most to the Sun's surface temperature, about 5800 K, whatever the lens.`],
  "Field of view & crop factor": [r`The angle of view follows from the sensor dimension and the focal length (for a rectilinear lens focused at infinity). The crop factor compares a sensor's diagonal with full frame's 43.27 mm, so focal lengths and f-numbers scale to the same picture.`, r`A 25 mm lens on Micro Four Thirds (crop 2) frames like a 50 mm on full frame: 47° diagonal.`],
  "Depth of field": [r`Points off the focus plane image as blur disks; depth of field is the range where they stay below the circle of confusion c. It grows with f-number and distance and shrinks with focal length, and is asymmetric: more behind the subject than in front.`, r`50 mm at f/2.8 focused at 3 m with c = 0.03 mm: sharp from 2.7 m to 3.3 m.`],
  "Hyperfocal distance": [r`Focusing at the hyperfocal distance puts the far limit of the depth of field at infinity and the near limit at half that distance: the largest possible sharp zone for a given aperture.`, r`24 mm at f/8 with c = 0.03 mm: H = 2.4 m, so everything from 1.2 m to infinity is acceptably sharp.`],
  "Depth of focus": [r`Image-side tolerance: how far the sensor may move from the true image plane before blur exceeds c. It grows with f-number and, at macro magnifications, with (1 + m). It sets flange-distance and sensor-flatness tolerances.`, r`At f/1.4 with 5 µm pixels (c ≈ 10 µm) the depth of focus is ±14 µm, less than the thickness of a hair.`],
  "Perspective & compression": [r`Relative sizes in an image depend only on distances from the camera. A long lens "compresses" a scene only because one stands further back; cropping a wide-angle image from the same spot gives the identical perspective.`, r`Two people 2 m apart look nearly the same size from 50 m (ratio 1.04) but very different from 2 m away (ratio 2).`],
  "Projection mappings (fisheye)": [r`A lens maps field angle θ to image height r. Rectilinear lenses keep straight lines straight but stretch the corners and cannot reach 180°; fisheyes use equidistant, equisolid-angle or stereographic mappings to cover a hemisphere.`, r`At θ = 80° a 10 mm rectilinear lens needs r = 56.7 mm; an equisolid fisheye puts the same ray at 12.9 mm.`],
  "Tilt (Scheimpflug) & hinge rule": [r`When the lens is tilted against the sensor, the plane of sharp focus tilts too. The sensor plane, the lens plane and the focus plane meet in one line (Scheimpflug), and the focus plane rotates about a hinge line at distance J = f/sin α below the lens.`, r`A 90 mm lens tilted 5° has J = 1.03 m: with the lens about 1 m above a table, the plane of focus can lie along the tabletop.`],
  "Shift & keystone correction": [r`Keeping the sensor parallel to a building keeps verticals parallel; shifting the lens moves the frame up within a larger image circle instead of tilting the camera.`, r`A 12 mm vertical shift of a 36 × 24 mm frame needs an image circle of 60 mm instead of 43.3 mm.`],
  "Bellows factor & pupil magnification": [r`At close range the image is further from the lens and the cone of light at the sensor narrows: the working f-number grows by (1 + |m|/m<sub>p</sub>), where m<sub>p</sub> is the ratio of exit to entrance pupil diameters.`, r`At 1:1 a symmetric macro lens (m<sub>p</sub> = 1) set to f/8 works at f/16 and needs two stops more exposure.`],
  "Focus stacking": [r`Each frame is sharp over a thin slab; merging frames by picking the locally sharpest pixel extends the depth of field without stopping down into diffraction. Frames must overlap and the perspective change between them must be corrected.`, r`At 2:1 and f/8 (working f/24, c = 0.03 mm) the depth of field is 0.36 mm, so a 5 mm deep insect needs about 18 frames with 20 % overlap.`],
  "Focus breathing": [r`Internally focusing lenses change their effective focal length as they focus, so the framing changes during a focus pull. Cinema lenses are designed to suppress it; many stills lenses lose 10–20 % of focal length at close focus.`, r`A 100 mm macro that frames at 1:1 may have an effective focal length near 65 mm there, so a focus pull visibly zooms.`],
  "Telecentricity": [r`If the exit pupil is at infinity, chief rays reach the sensor parallel to the axis. Image size then does not change with small focus errors, and every pixel sees the same cone of light: vital for metrology, microlens sensors and colour filters.`, r`Object-space telecentric lenses for machine vision need a front element at least as large as the field they measure.`],
  "Fermat's principle": [r`Among neighbouring paths, light takes one whose optical length \(\int n\,ds\) is stationary (usually a minimum). Snell's law, reflection and the perfect imaging of an ellipse or paraboloid all follow, and in an image every ray from a point has the same optical path length.`, r`A paraboloid focuses an axial plane wave perfectly because all paths from the wavefront to the focus have equal length.`],
  "Lagrange invariant": [r`In paraxial optics \(\mathcal H = n(\bar u y - u\bar y)\) is the same at every surface. It is proportional to the étendue of the system, sets the information capacity of the lens and appears in every Seidel coefficient of field curvature.`, r`Doubling both the field angle and the aperture of a design quadruples \(\mathcal H\) and makes it far harder to correct.`],
  "Vergence & dioptres": [r`Optometry works with reciprocal distances: a bundle's vergence V = n/s and a lens's power P = n/f in dioptres (m⁻¹). Imaging then becomes addition, V' = V + P, and thin lenses in contact simply add.`, r`Reading at 33 cm needs 3 D of accommodation; a presbyope with 1 D left needs +2 D reading glasses.`],
  "Image circle & coverage": [r`The image circle is where illumination and sharpness are still acceptable. Lenses for larger formats, or with movements, need larger circles; adapting a lens to a smaller format crops it.`, r`Full frame needs 43.3 mm; a 4 × 5 inch view camera lens covering 150 mm or more allows generous tilts and shifts.`],
  "Pinhole camera": [r`A large hole projects its own shadow (blur ≈ d), a small one diffracts (blur ≈ 2.44 λf/d). The sum is smallest when the two are comparable, at \(d\approx1.9\sqrt{f\lambda}\) (Rayleigh), with infinite depth of field.`, r`For f = 100 mm at 550 nm the optimum pinhole is 0.45 mm, an f-number of about f/220.`],
  // ── Monochromatic aberrations ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Spherical aberration": [r`Zones of a spherical surface have different focal lengths: marginal rays focus nearer the lens than paraxial ones in a positive singlet. The image of a point has a sharp core and a halo; the best compromise focus lies between the paraxial and marginal foci.`, r`A 50 mm f/2 plano-convex lens in BK7, curved side forward, focuses its marginal rays 3.6 mm in front of the paraxial focus, far beyond its depth of focus.`],
  "Coma": [r`Off axis, annular zones of the pupil image with different magnifications, so a point becomes a comet with its tail pointing away from (or towards) the centre. Its wavefront term grows linearly with field and as the cube of the aperture, the blur as the square of the aperture, so it dominates fast lenses at the edges: stars become "seagulls".`, r`Wide-open f/1.4 lenses often show coma at the corners in astrophotography; stopping down to f/2.8 shrinks the comatic blur about fourfold.`],
  "Astigmatism": [r`For an oblique bundle the tangential and sagittal fans have different focal lengths, giving two line foci at right angles with a round "circle of least confusion" between them. It grows as the square of field angle.`, r`A point at the corner of a cheap wide-angle lens may appear as a radial line at one focus and a tangential line at another.`],
  "Field curvature (Petzval)": [r`Even with astigmatism removed, the image of a flat object lies on a curved Petzval surface, whose curvature depends only on the powers and indices of the elements. Flattening it needs negative power separated from positive power, or a curved sensor.`, r`A single lens of f = 100 mm and n = 1.5 has a Petzval radius of −150 mm: at 10 mm off-axis the focus is 0.33 mm away.`],
  "Distortion": [r`Distortion moves image points without blurring them: magnification varies with field. Barrel distortion bends straight lines outward (wide-angles, zooms at the short end), pincushion inward (telephotos). Software corrects it by resampling, at a small loss of resolution at the corners.`, r`A 3 % barrel distortion moves a corner point of a 6000 px wide image by about 100 px.`],
  "Decentering & tilt (tolerances)": [r`Elements displaced or tilted by a few micrometres break rotational symmetry: coma appears on axis, astigmatism and field curvature become asymmetric and one side of the image is softer. Brown–Conrady terms model the resulting tangential distortion.`, r`A 20 µm decentre of a strong element in a fast prime can noticeably soften one side of the frame.`],
  "Focus shift on stopping down": [r`With residual spherical aberration, best focus depends on which zones of the pupil are used. Focus set wide open (where autofocus works) drifts when the lens stops down for the exposure.`, r`Some fast 50 mm lenses shift best focus by tens of micrometres between f/1.4 and f/2.8, enough to miss critical focus at close range.`],
  "Higher-order aberrations": [r`Beyond third order come fifth-order terms such as secondary spherical, oblique spherical and elliptical coma. Designers balance them against third-order terms of opposite sign, which is why fast and wide lenses need many elements and aspheres.`, r`A fast lens balances third-order spherical aberration against fifth-order of the opposite sign: the marginal ray is corrected and a zonal residual remains near 0.7 of the aperture.`],
  "Pupil aberration": [r`The pupils are themselves images and are aberrated. In retrofocus wide-angles the entrance pupil grows and tilts towards off-axis objects, which partly offsets the cos⁴ law and is why such lenses vignette less than expected.`, r`The corner of a 14 mm lens on full frame is 57° off axis, where cos⁴ alone predicts a loss of 3.5 stops; such lenses lose markedly less.`],
  "Wavefront error & Strehl ratio": [r`Aberrations are departures of the wavefront from a sphere. The peak intensity relative to a perfect system, the Strehl ratio, follows from the RMS error by the Maréchal approximation, valid for small errors. A Strehl of 0.8 (λ/14 RMS) is the usual criterion for "diffraction-limited".`, r`An RMS error of 40 nm at 550 nm gives S ≈ 0.81; Hubble's flawed mirror had about 0.3 µm RMS and S ≈ 0.1.`],
  "Transverse ray aberration": [r`The position where each ray hits the image plane follows from the slope of the wave aberration at its pupil point. Spot diagrams and ray-fan plots are this gradient, and geometric blur grows with the derivatives of W.`, r`Defocus W = W₀₂₀ρ² gives a uniform blur disk of radius 4 N W₀₂₀ at the image.`],
  "Aspheric surfaces": [r`A conic constant and polynomial terms let one surface cancel spherical aberration that would otherwise need several spherical elements. Moulded and ground aspheres are in almost every modern lens; small polishing ripples on them cause onion-ring bokeh.`, r`A paraboloid (k = −1) images an axial point at infinity with no spherical aberration at any aperture.`],
  "Seidel sums": [r`To third order, every aberration of a system is a sum of surface contributions computed from two paraxial rays: S<sub>I</sub> (spherical), S<sub>II</sub> (coma), S<sub>III</sub> (astigmatism), S<sub>IV</sub> (Petzval) and S<sub>V</sub> (distortion). Designers read off which surfaces cause trouble and how a stop or bending change will help.`, r`For Hubble's two mirrors the sums show that a Cassegrain's coma equals that of a paraboloid of the same 57.6 m focal length.`],
  "Zernike polynomials": [r`Zernike polynomials are orthogonal over the unit disk, so the coefficients of a wavefront fit are independent and their squares add to the variance. Noll's normalisation and ordering are the standard in astronomy and in interferometric testing.`, r`A wavefront with 30 nm RMS of astigmatism and 40 nm RMS of coma has 50 nm RMS in total.`],
  "Abbe sine condition & aplanatism": [r`A lens corrected for spherical aberration is also free of coma near the axis if the ratio of the sines of conjugate marginal-ray angles is constant (the offence against the sine condition, OSC, is zero). Such a system is aplanatic.`, r`Microscope objectives obey the sine condition to keep the whole field sharp at NA 0.95.`],
  "Coddington equations": [r`Coddington's equations trace the tangential and sagittal foci of a thin bundle along a real chief ray through each surface. They give astigmatism and field curvature exactly at any field angle, not only to third order.`, r`For a distant object, a single surface puts the tangential focus at cos²I′ of the sagittal distance: 20 % closer for a refraction angle I′ = 26.6°.`],
  "Stop shift": [r`If spherical aberration is present, moving the stop changes which part of each surface the oblique beams use, and with it coma, astigmatism and distortion. The stop-shift formulas give the new sums from the eccentricity E = δȳ/y.`, r`The landscape lens places its stop in front of a meniscus to flatten the field and control coma.`],
  "Ritchey–Chrétien aplanat": [r`Choosing both mirror conics of a Cassegrain to zero spherical aberration and coma gives an aplanat with a field limited only by astigmatism and field curvature. Almost every large telescope since the 1960s, Hubble included, is an RC or a three-mirror descendant.`, r`For Hubble's layout (f/2.3 primary, f/24 system) the conics are K₁ = −1.0023 and K₂ = −1.497.`],
  // ── Chromatic effects ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Dispersion (Sellmeier)": [r`The index of glass falls with wavelength because the visible lies between ultraviolet electronic resonances and infrared lattice resonances. The Sellmeier equation models these as oscillators and fits catalogue data to about 10⁻⁶.`, r`N-BK7: n = 1.5224 at 486 nm, 1.5168 at 588 nm and 1.5143 at 656 nm.`],
  "Abbe number": [r`V<sub>d</sub> compares the refracting power (n<sub>d</sub> − 1) with the dispersion between the F and C lines. Crowns have high V (low dispersion), flints low V. The focal spread of a singlet is about f/V.`, r`N-BK7 has V<sub>d</sub> = 64.2, F2 flint 36.4, calcium fluoride about 95.`],
  "Longitudinal chromatic aberration (LoCA)": [r`Blue light focuses closer to the lens than red. In front of and behind the focus plane, out-of-focus highlights get coloured rims, typically magenta on one side and green on the other ("bokeh fringing"). Stopping down reduces the blur, not the focus spread.`, r`A 100 mm BK7 singlet focuses F and C light 1.6 mm apart.`],
  "Lateral chromatic aberration (LaCA)": [r`When magnification depends on wavelength, the colour images have different sizes: edges towards the corners get red and blue fringes that do not change with aperture. Because it is a geometric scaling, raw converters correct it well.`, r`A 0.05 % magnification difference shifts the colour channels by about 2 pixels at the corner of a 24 MP image.`],
  "Achromat condition": [r`Two thin lenses in contact of different glasses have the same focal length at two wavelengths if their powers are weighted by the reciprocal Abbe numbers. The crown carries the positive power, the flint a weaker negative power.`, r`f = 100 mm from BK7 and F2: f₁ = +43.3 mm and f₂ = −76.4 mm.`],
  "Secondary spectrum & apochromats": [r`An achromat brings two colours together but leaves the others on a curve: the secondary spectrum, about f/2000 for normal glasses. Apochromats use glasses with anomalous partial dispersion (fluorite, ED glass) to bring three colours together.`, r`A 1000 mm f/8 achromatic refractor leaves about 0.5 mm of secondary spectrum, a purple halo around bright stars.`],
  "Spherochromatism": [r`Spherical aberration varies with wavelength because the index does. A lens corrected for spherical aberration in green is under-corrected in blue and over-corrected in red, so out-of-focus highlights show coloured rings even when the focus is right.`, r`Fast portrait lenses often show green-rimmed bokeh behind the subject and magenta in front for this reason combined with LoCA.`],
  "Purple fringing": [r`Purple halos at backlit edges come from several sources: short-wavelength LoCA, where lenses are least corrected, light spreading in the sensor stack and microlenses, and blooming from saturated pixels. It is strongest wide open on high-contrast edges.`, r`Branches against a bright sky often show 2–5 pixels of violet fringe at f/1.4 that largely disappear by f/4.`],
  "Atmospheric dispersion": [r`The atmosphere is a weak prism: refraction increases towards blue, so a star near the horizon is stretched into a short vertical spectrum. Large telescopes correct it with rotating prisms (ADCs) in front of the instrument.`, r`At sea level and 60° from the zenith, 400 nm and 700 nm are separated by 2.6″, several times a diffraction-limited image.`],
  "Rainbows, prisms & halos": [r`Light refracted into a water drop, reflected once and refracted out has a minimum deviation that piles up light at about 42° from the antisolar point, with red outside. Hexagonal ice crystals act as 60° prisms, giving the 22° halo.`, r`Water (n = 1.333) gives a primary bow at 42°; ice (n = 1.31) gives the 22° halo; the secondary bow at 51° has its colours reversed.`],
  "Partial dispersion & the glass map": [r`Partial dispersion measures how the dispersion is shared between the blue and red ends of the spectrum. Ordinary glasses lie near a straight "normal line"; a two-glass achromat of such glasses always keeps a secondary spectrum. Glasses well off the line make apochromats possible.`, r`Fluorite and FPL-type ED glasses (V ≈ 80–95) lie well above the normal line, which is why they appear in almost every apochromatic telephoto.`],
  "Ocular chromatic aberration": [r`The eye is a water-filled lens without colour correction: blue focuses about 2 D in front of red. The macular pigment absorbs much of the blue, cones are sparse in it, and the brain discounts the rest, so it is rarely noticed.`, r`If red text on a blue background seems to float at a different depth ("chromostereopsis"), this is the cause.`],
  // ── Diffraction and wave optics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Diffraction gratings & resolving power": [r`A grating sends each wavelength into the direction where light from all grooves adds in phase. Its resolving power equals the order times the number of illuminated grooves, so large gratings at high orders resolve fine spectral lines.`, r`600 lines/mm in first order sends 500 nm to 17.5°; a 50 mm wide grating has R = 30 000, resolving 0.017 nm.`],
  "Blaze & order overlap": [r`Sawtooth grooves tilted at the blaze angle concentrate most light in one order. Higher orders overlap: λ in order m falls where λ·m/(m+1) does in order m+1, so the usable range per order (the free spectral range) is λ/m and order-sorting filters or cross-dispersers are needed.`, r`An échelle used in order 50 at 500 nm has a free spectral range of only 10 nm.`],
  "Prism spectroscopy": [r`A prism separates colours by dispersion of its glass. Its resolving power depends only on the base length and dn/dλ, not on the apex angle. Prisms waste no light in other orders but their dispersion is non-linear (strong in the blue).`, r`A BK7 prism with a 50 mm base gives R ≈ 2500 at 500 nm; dense flints do about three times better.`],
  "Airy disk": [r`A uniformly illuminated circular aperture images a point as a bright disk surrounded by faint rings; the first dark ring is at 1.22 λN. It is the smallest spot any lens of that f-number can make, so it sets a floor on blur.`, r`At f/8 and 550 nm the first dark ring has a radius of 5.4 µm, larger than many pixels.`],
  "Rayleigh, Sparrow & Dawes limits": [r`Two equally bright points count as resolved when the maximum of one falls on the first zero of the other (Rayleigh), when the dip between them just vanishes (Sparrow), or by Dawes' empirical rule for double stars. All are conventions; deconvolution and known PSFs can do better.`, r`A 200 mm telescope at 550 nm: Rayleigh 0.69″, Dawes 0.58″.`],
  "Diffraction-limited aperture": [r`As the lens stops down, the Airy disk grows until it covers more than a pixel; beyond that f-number further stopping down visibly softens fine detail. The transition is gradual because the MTFs of the lens and of the pixel multiply.`, r`With 4 µm pixels the Airy radius equals a pixel at about f/6.`],
  "Diffraction spikes (spiders, struts)": [r`A straight, thin obstruction in the pupil diffracts light perpendicular to itself: its Fourier transform is a line. Each strut gives a spike pair, so a four-vane spider gives four spikes and three struts give six.`, r`Hubble's four-vane spider gives four spikes; JWST's segments and three struts give six large spikes and two smaller ones.`],
  "Sunstars (iris starbursts)": [r`The straight edges of a polygonal aperture each diffract into a pair of spikes. With an even number of blades the pairs overlap (n spikes); with an odd number they interleave (2n spikes). Rounded blades give weaker, fanned spikes.`, r`An 8-blade iris gives 8 spikes, a 7-blade iris 14.`],
  "Segmented-mirror PSF": [r`A segmented pupil is one segment shape repeated on a lattice, so its PSF is the PSF of one segment multiplied by the interference pattern of the lattice: sharp peaks inside a broad envelope, plus spikes from the hexagon edges.`, r`JWST's 18 hexagonal segments of 1.32 m produce its characteristic six-pointed "snowflake" PSF.`],
  "Central obstruction": [r`A secondary mirror blocks the centre of the pupil. For the same collected power, the peak drops to (1 − ε²) of an unobstructed pupil, the core narrows slightly and the rings brighten. The MTF loses contrast at middle frequencies.`, r`With Hubble's ε = 0.33 the normalised peak is 0.89 of that of an unobstructed 2.4 m aperture, and the first ring is noticeably brighter.`],
  "Optical transfer function (MTF)": [r`The MTF is the contrast with which a lens reproduces sine patterns, as a function of spatial frequency. It is the Fourier transform of the PSF, or equivalently the autocorrelation of the pupil function, and the MTFs of lens, sensor and processing multiply.`, r`A perfect f/8 lens at 550 nm has MTF 0.72 at 50 lp/mm and reaches zero at 227 lp/mm.`],
  "Diffraction cut-off frequency": [r`The pupil autocorrelation vanishes beyond a shift of one pupil diameter, so no lens transmits spatial frequencies above 1/(λN) in the image. It is a hard limit, unlike the Rayleigh criterion.`, r`At f/16 and 550 nm nothing above 114 lp/mm passes: a 36 MP full-frame sensor samples up to 102 lp/mm.`],
  "Defocus OTF & spurious resolution": [r`A defocused lens blurs points into disks whose transfer function is an Airy-like function of frequency. Beyond its first zero it goes negative: fine stripes reappear with inverted contrast ("spurious resolution"), and out-of-focus Siemens stars show phase reversals.`, r`A 50 µm blur disk has its first zero at 24 lp/mm; finer stripes reappear inverted.`],
  "Fresnel diffraction & defocus rings": [r`When the Fresnel number of the aperture seen from the observation point is not small, the diffraction pattern depends on distance: the centre of the beam alternates between bright and dark as the number of Fresnel zones changes. A defocused star seen in a telescope shows these rings.`, r`A 0.5 mm hole seen from 1 m in green light has a Fresnel number of 0.45: nearly far-field.`],
  "Apodisation": [r`A pupil whose transmission tapers towards the edge has a PSF with weaker rings, at the cost of a slightly wider core and some light. Photographic "smooth trans focus" lenses use an apodising filter for smooth bokeh; coronagraphs use extreme apodisation.`, r`Sony's 100 mm f/2.8 STF lens transmits like T5.6 because of its apodisation filter.`],
  "Poisson–Arago spot": [r`For a round obstacle, Fresnel diffraction gives a bright point on the axis of the shadow as bright as the unobstructed light, because the edge contributions from all around the disk arrive in phase. Its observation by Arago in 1818 settled the wave theory of light.`, r`Behind a 5 mm disk at 1 m in red laser light the spot has a radius of about 0.1 mm.`],
  "Babinet's principle": [r`The fields diffracted by an aperture and by its complementary screen add up to the unobstructed field, so away from the direct beam their diffraction patterns are identical. Measuring the diffraction pattern of a hair is as good as measuring a slit of the same width.`, r`An 80 µm hair 2 m from the screen in red laser light gives fringes 16 mm apart.`],
  "Stellar interferometry (van Cittert–Zernike)": [r`Light from a partially resolved source is partially coherent: the visibility of fringes between two apertures is the Fourier transform of the source's brightness. For a uniform disk the visibility first vanishes at baseline B = 1.22 λ/θ, which measures the angular diameter.`, r`Michelson and Pease (1920) saw the fringes of Betelgeuse vanish at a 3.07 m baseline: θ ≈ 0.047″.`],
  "Aperture synthesis & CLEAN": [r`Each pair of telescopes measures one Fourier component of the sky. Earth's rotation sweeps the baselines across the u-v plane; the inverse transform is the true image convolved with the "dirty beam" of the incomplete sampling, which CLEAN and newer methods deconvolve.`, r`The Event Horizon Telescope at 1.3 mm with Earth-sized baselines reaches about 25 µas, enough to image the shadow of M87*.`],
  "Coherence & speckle": [r`Coherent light scattered from a rough surface, or passed through turbulence, interferes randomly: a grainy speckle pattern with grains about the size of the diffraction limit. It limits laser imaging and makes short astronomical exposures look like clusters of dots.`, r`Laser light viewed through an f/8 aperture forms speckle grains about λN ≈ 5 µm across on the sensor.`],
  "Thin-film interference": [r`Reflections from the top and bottom of a thin layer interfere; the colour seen depends on thickness, index and angle. Oil films, soap bubbles and Newton's rings show it; lens coatings exploit it.`, r`A soap film turns black just before it bursts, when it is much thinner than a quarter wave (about 100 nm).`],
  "Encircled energy": [r`The fraction of the PSF's power within a radius is the relevant quantity for photometry and spectrograph slits. For the Airy pattern it has a closed form: 84 % inside the first dark ring, 91 % inside the second.`, r`An f/10 telescope at 550 nm concentrates 84 % of a star's light in a 13.4 µm wide disk.`],
  "Gaussian beams": [r`A laser's fundamental mode keeps a Gaussian profile everywhere. Its width is smallest at the waist and grows by √2 over the Rayleigh range; far away it diverges at λ/(πw₀). Lenses transform waists rather than imaging points.`, r`A HeNe beam with a 0.4 mm waist has a Rayleigh range of 0.79 m and diverges at 0.5 mrad.`],
  "Fresnel zone plates": [r`Blocking every other Fresnel zone leaves only contributions that arrive in phase at a point, which therefore becomes a focus. The focal length is r₁²/λ, so zone plates are strongly chromatic; they are the lenses of X-ray microscopes and of photon-sieve telescope concepts.`, r`r₁ = 0.5 mm gives f = 0.45 m at 550 nm and 22 % more at 450 nm.`],
  "Talbot self-imaging": [r`The field behind a periodic grating repeats itself at distances that are multiples of the Talbot length, with shifted and fractional images between. Talbot interferometers measure phase gradients without lenses, including in X-ray imaging.`, r`A 100 µm grating in 633 nm light re-images itself every 31.6 mm.`],
  // ── Radiometry and illumination ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Camera equation": [r`Image irradiance depends on scene radiance, lens transmission and f-number only, not on distance (for distant objects), with the cos⁴ factor off axis. This is why exposure settings carry over from one subject distance to another.`, r`A surface of 1000 cd/m² through an f/4 lens with 90 % transmission gives about 44 lux on the sensor at the centre.`],
  "Exposure value": [r`Exposure value combines aperture and time on a base-2 scale: each EV step halves or doubles the light. At a fixed ISO, a scene's light level corresponds to one EV, and all equivalent aperture-time pairs share it.`, r`f/16 at 1/125 s is EV 15, a sunny day at ISO 100; f/2 at 1/30 s is EV 7, a bright interior.`],
  "T-stops": [r`An f-number describes geometry only; lenses lose 10–30 % of the light in glass and coatings. Cinema lenses are marked in T-stops, so that switching lenses at the same T-stop keeps exposure identical.`, r`An f/1.4 lens transmitting 80 % works at T1.57.`],
  "Natural vignetting (cos⁴)": [r`An oblique bundle sees the pupil foreshortened (cos θ), travels further (cos² θ) and strikes the sensor obliquely (cos θ). The product, cos⁴ θ, is a law for thin lenses; real lenses depart from it through pupil aberration.`, r`At 30° off axis cos⁴ gives 0.56 (0.8 stop); at 45°, 0.25 (2 stops).`],
  "Mechanical vignetting": [r`Off axis, the front and rear barrel openings cut into the beam, so the effective pupil becomes a lens-shaped intersection. It darkens the corners of fast lenses wide open and gives cat's-eye bokeh; stopping down by one to two stops removes it.`, r`A 50 mm f/1.4 may lose 1.5–2 stops at the corners wide open, most of it mechanical.`],
  "Pixel vignetting": [r`Microlenses and the depth of the pixel well accept light only up to some angle. Steep chief rays at the edges of the frame lose light and spill into neighbours. Sensors with shifted microlenses, and lens designs with far exit pupils, avoid it.`, r`Wide-angle rangefinder lenses with chief rays at 30° need offset microlenses on digital Leica M sensors.`],
  "Inverse-square law": [r`Irradiance from a point source falls as 1/d² because the same power spreads over a sphere of area 4πd². Flash guide numbers encode it: the product of distance and f-number is constant for correct exposure.`, r`A guide number of 40 (metres, ISO 100) exposes a subject at 5 m correctly at f/8.`],
  "Lambertian reflection": [r`A perfectly matte surface reflects with radiance independent of direction; its luminance is the illuminance times albedo divided by π. Real surfaces are only approximately Lambertian; the Moon's limb, for example, is not darkened as Lambert would predict.`, r`Fresh snow (albedo 0.9) under 100 000 lux has a luminance of about 29 000 cd/m².`],
  "Surface-brightness conservation": [r`An optical system can magnify an extended object but cannot make its surface brighter; the same applies to gravitational lenses. Larger telescopes show extended objects bigger, not brighter, at the same exit-pupil size.`, r`The Andromeda galaxy's surface is no brighter in a 1 m telescope than in binoculars; only point sources gain.`],
  "Film characteristic curve & grain": [r`Silver-halide grains become developable after absorbing a few photons, so the developed density is an S-shaped function of log exposure (Hurter–Driffield curve). Faster films use larger grains: more sensitivity, more grain. Granularity follows Selwyn's law.`, r`Halving the measuring aperture's diameter doubles the measured density fluctuation σ<sub>D</sub>, as σ<sub>D</sub>√A = constant predicts.`],
  "Reciprocity failure": [r`At very low light levels a grain must collect its photons before the latent image decays, so long exposures are less efficient than their nominal time (Schwarzschild exponent p below 1). Digital sensors are linear and do not suffer from it.`, r`With p = 0.8 a metered 100 s exposure needs about 316 s.`],
  "Flat-field calibration": [r`Dividing by an image of a uniform source, after subtracting the dark frame, removes vignetting, dust shadows and pixel-to-pixel sensitivity variations. It is a standard step in astronomical and scientific imaging.`, r`Dust on a sensor cover glass casts out-of-focus donuts a few per cent deep that a good flat field removes completely.`],
  "Photometric units": [r`Photometry weights radiometric quantities by the eye's luminous efficiency V(λ). Luminous flux is in lumens, illuminance in lux (lm/m²), luminance in cd/m². The maximum luminous efficacy is 683 lm/W at 555 nm.`, r`Full sunlight gives about 100 000 lux, a lit office 500 lux, full moonlight 0.25 lux.`],
  "Exposure metering & sunny 16": [r`Reflected-light meters assume the scene averages to a mid-grey of about 12–18 % reflectance and set N²/t accordingly. Very bright or very dark scenes therefore need compensation. The sunny-16 rule is the same equation for direct sunlight.`, r`At ISO 100 in sunshine: f/16 at 1/100 s, or equivalently f/8 at 1/400 s.`],
  "Blackbody radiation": [r`An ideal thermal emitter radiates Planck's spectrum, whose peak wavelength falls inversely with temperature. Real thermal sources (the Sun, tungsten) are close to it, and colour temperature describes other sources by the blackbody whose colour they match.`, r`The Sun at 5800 K peaks near 500 nm; a 2856 K tungsten lamp peaks at 1.01 µm, in the infrared.`],
  "Astronomical magnitudes": [r`Magnitudes are a logarithmic flux scale inherited from Hipparchus: 5 magnitudes are a factor of exactly 100, brighter objects have smaller magnitudes, and a zero point ties the scale to photon rates in each filter band.`, r`A 20th-magnitude galaxy is 10⁸ times fainter than Vega (magnitude 0).`],
  // ── Stray light ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Fresnel reflection": [r`At normal incidence an uncoated surface reflects ((n₁ − n₂)/(n₁ + n₂))² of the light, rising steeply at grazing angles. Light lost to reflection becomes stray light, so every glass-air surface of a lens matters.`, r`An uncoated n = 1.5 surface reflects 4 %; dense glass with n = 1.9 reflects 9.6 %.`],
  "Ghost images": [r`Light reflected twice inside a lens, once back and once forward again, forms out-of-focus images of a bright source and of the aperture. They lie on the line through the source and the image centre. Their number grows as the square of the number of surfaces.`, r`A lens with 10 air-glass surfaces has 45 double-reflection ghost paths.`],
  "Anti-reflection coatings": [r`A quarter-wave layer of index √(n₀n<sub>s</sub>) cancels the reflection at one wavelength; multilayer stacks broaden the band and nanostructured coatings grade the index. Modern multicoatings reduce surface reflections to well below 0.5 %.`, r`A single MgF₂ layer (n = 1.38) 100 nm thick on crown glass leaves about 1.3 % reflection at 550 nm.`],
  "Veiling glare": [r`Light scattered by surfaces, coatings, the barrel and the sensor spreads a low-level haze over the whole image. It adds a nearly uniform offset, so it hurts shadows most and limits the usable dynamic range of the lens itself.`, r`A glare level of 1 % of the average luminance cuts a 1000:1 scene to a few hundred to one in the shadows.`],
  "Sensor (red-dot) flare": [r`The regular structure of the sensor (microlenses, pixels) acts as a reflective grating. Light reflected from it is diffracted back to the lens, reflected again and returns as a grid of coloured dots, often red because of the IR filter's reflection band.`, r`A 4.9 µm pixel pitch diffracts 650 nm light into orders 7.6° apart.`],
  "Filter ghosts": [r`A flat filter in front of the lens reflects light from the sensor and lens back towards the sensor. Being nearly in focus, the ghost is a sharp, inverted copy of bright sources mirrored through the image centre.`, r`Street lights at night often appear doubled point-symmetrically when a UV filter is used.`],
  "Surface-roughness scatter": [r`Polishing leaves nanometre-scale roughness that scatters light out of the image; the total integrated scatter grows as (σ/λ)², so it matters most in the ultraviolet and for coronagraphs.`, r`A surface with 1 nm RMS roughness scatters 0.05 % of 550 nm light.`],
  "Baffles & stray-light suppression": [r`Telescopes use baffle tubes, vanes, black coatings, Lyot stops and field stops so that no direct path exists from bright sources to the detector. Stray-light analysis traces billions of rays and expresses the result as the point-source transmittance.`, r`JWST's five-layer sunshield cuts the Sun's 200 kW to below a watt reaching the cold side.`],
  "Total internal reflection": [r`Going from glass to air beyond the critical angle, no light is transmitted (only an evanescent wave crosses a thin gap). Prism binoculars, SLR pentaprisms and optical fibres rely on it because it is lossless.`, r`For n = 1.5 the critical angle is 41.8°, so a 45° prism face reflects totally.`],
  "Lens hoods": [r`A hood blocks light from outside the field of view that would otherwise hit the front element and scatter or form ghosts. Its depth is limited by the diagonal field of view; petal shapes follow the rectangular frame.`, r`A 24 mm lens on full frame sees 84° diagonally, so its hood must be short and petal-shaped.`],
  "Dust & scratches on optics": [r`Defects on a lens far from the focus are completely out of focus; they only remove or scatter a fraction of light equal to their share of the pupil. They matter against bright sources, where scattered light becomes flare.`, r`A 0.1 mm dust grain on a 50 mm diameter front element blocks 4 × 10⁻⁶ of the light: invisible.`],
  // ── Polarisation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Brewster's angle": [r`At Brewster's angle the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular, and the p-polarised component is not reflected at all. Reflections near this angle are strongly s-polarised, which is why polarisers remove glare from water and glass.`, r`For water (n = 1.333) Brewster's angle is 53°; for glass (n = 1.5), 56°.`],
  "Malus's law (polarisers)": [r`An ideal linear polariser transmits the cosine-squared projection of a polarised beam and half of an unpolarised one. Two crossed polarisers transmit nothing, except where something in between rotates the polarisation.`, r`Polarised light through a polariser at 60° keeps 25 % of its intensity.`],
  "Sky polarisation": [r`Light scattered by molecules is polarised perpendicular to the scattering plane, most strongly at 90° from the Sun. A polariser darkens that band of sky, and the effect fades towards the Sun and the antisolar point.`, r`A clear sky is up to about 75 % polarised 90° from the Sun, less with haze.`],
  "Birefringence": [r`Anisotropic crystals have two indices; a ray splits into ordinary and extraordinary rays with orthogonal polarisations that walk apart. Camera OLPFs use quartz or lithium niobate plates to split each point into two or four.`, r`Quartz cut at 45° displaces the extraordinary ray by 5.9 µm per mm of thickness.`],
  "Stress birefringence": [r`Stress makes glass anisotropic, with an index difference proportional to the stress difference (photoelasticity). It degrades lenses mounted too tightly, and between crossed polarisers it shows stress patterns as coloured fringes.`, r`10 MPa in BK7 (K = 2.8 × 10⁻¹² Pa⁻¹) gives 280 nm of retardance over 10 mm: about half a wave.`],
  "Polarisation aberrations": [r`Coatings, oblique reflections and stressed glass change the amplitude and phase of each polarisation differently across the pupil. Each polarisation then has a slightly different PSF; for coronagraphs at 10⁻¹⁰ contrast this is a dominant error.`, r`Fold mirrors in coronagraphs are often kept at small angles of incidence, and some designs keep a single polarisation.`],
  "Fresnel equations": [r`The amplitude reflection and transmission coefficients for s and p polarisation follow from the boundary conditions of Maxwell's equations. They give the 4 % reflection at normal incidence, Brewster's angle for p, and total internal reflection beyond the critical angle.`, r`From glass at 60° incidence, s light is reflected at 17.7 % and p light at 0.2 %.`],
  "Stokes parameters & Mueller matrices": [r`Four measurable intensities (I, Q, U, V) describe any beam, polarised, unpolarised or partially polarised. Each element (polariser, waveplate, reflection) is a 4 × 4 Mueller matrix acting on the Stokes vector, which makes polarimetry a linear-algebra problem.`, r`Skylight with Q = 0.5 I and U = 0 is 50 % linearly polarised along a horizontal or vertical axis.`],
  "Waveplates & retardance": [r`A birefringent plate delays one polarisation relative to the other. A half-wave plate rotates linear polarisation by twice its axis angle; a quarter-wave plate at 45° converts linear into circular light.`, r`A zero-order quarter-wave plate of quartz (Δn = 0.0091) is 16 µm thick at 590 nm.`],
  "Circular polarisers (cameras)": [r`A camera "circular polariser" is a linear polariser followed by a quarter-wave plate. It removes glare like a linear polariser but emits circular light, which the beam splitters of autofocus and metering systems handle without error.`, r`Like any polariser it costs about 1.3–1.5 stops of light with unpolarised scenes.`],
  // ── Sensors, sampling and noise ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Nyquist sampling & aliasing": [r`A grid of pixels with pitch p can represent frequencies only up to 1/(2p). Finer detail is not lost but folded back to a false lower frequency, indistinguishable from real detail; only blurring before sampling (lens or OLPF) prevents it.`, r`4 µm pixels have a Nyquist frequency of 125 lp/mm; a 150 lp/mm pattern appears as 100 lp/mm.`],
  "LED-wall moiré & refresh banding": [r`On a virtual-production stage the LED wall is itself a fine grid. When its image on the sensor approaches the pixel pitch it aliases into moiré, unless the wall is defocused. LED pulse-width modulation also bands in rolling-shutter cameras when the exposure is not a whole number of PWM periods.`, r`A 2.6 mm wall pitch imaged by a 50 mm lens from 5 m is 26 µm on the sensor, safely resolved; from 25 m it is 5 µm, close to the pixel pitch.`],
  "Moiré": [r`Two fine periodic patterns, such as fabric and the pixel grid, beat against each other and produce large false patterns at their difference frequency. In two dimensions the false pattern can also be rotated.`, r`Shirt fabric, distant brick walls and screens photographed close-up are classic sources.`],
  "Bayer CFA & false colour": [r`A colour filter array samples green at every other pixel and red and blue at every fourth. Red and blue alias at half the frequency of luminance, so fine black-and-white detail can come out coloured after demosaicing.`, r`With 4 µm pixels, red and blue alias above 62.5 lp/mm, half the sensor's luminance Nyquist frequency.`],
  "Optical low-pass filter": [r`Two birefringent plates split each point into four copies one pixel apart. The resulting MTF has its first zero at the Nyquist frequency, suppressing aliasing at the cost of some sharpness. Many high-resolution cameras omit it and rely on the lens.`, r`An OLPF with a 4 µm split has MTF |cos(πdν)| = 0 at 125 lp/mm.`],
  "Pixel aperture & fill factor": [r`A pixel integrates light over its area, which multiplies the system MTF by a sinc function. With microlenses the effective aperture is close to the pitch, giving 64 % contrast at the Nyquist frequency even with a perfect lens.`, r`A 100 % fill-factor pixel has MTF sinc(0.5) = 0.64 at Nyquist and zero at twice Nyquist.`],
  "Photon shot noise": [r`Photon arrivals are random, following Poisson statistics, so a signal of n photoelectrons fluctuates by √n. It is the dominant noise in well-exposed images and cannot be removed by any sensor; only collecting more light helps.`, r`A pixel with 10 000 e⁻ has SNR 100; one with 100 e⁻ has SNR 10.`],
  "Read noise": [r`Each readout adds electronic noise from the source follower, amplifiers and ADC, independent of signal. It dominates in deep shadows and in short exposures; modern CMOS sensors reach 1–3 e⁻, scientific CMOS below 1 e⁻.`, r`A shadow pixel with 4 e⁻ and 2 e⁻ of read noise has SNR 4/√(4 + 4) = 1.4.`],
  "Dark current & hot pixels": [r`Thermal energy generates electrons without light; the rate grows exponentially with temperature and varies from pixel to pixel, giving hot pixels. Astronomical cameras are cooled and subtract dark frames.`, r`Doubling every 6 °C, dark current rises eightfold between 20 °C and 38 °C.`],
  "Fixed-pattern noise (PRNU/DSNU)": [r`Pixels differ slightly in sensitivity (photo-response non-uniformity) and offset (dark-signal non-uniformity). The resulting noise grows linearly with signal and therefore dominates shot noise in bright areas unless flat-fielded.`, r`With PRNU of 1 %, fixed-pattern noise exceeds shot noise above 10 000 e⁻.`],
  "Full-well & dynamic range": [r`A pixel holds only so many electrons before clipping; the ratio of that full well to the noise floor is the sensor's dynamic range. Larger pixels and dual-gain designs extend it.`, r`A full well of 50 000 e⁻ and 3 e⁻ of read noise give 14 stops.`],
  "ISO & ISO invariance": [r`ISO sets the gain applied before the ADC; it does not change the photon count. When the read noise after the gain is small, raising ISO in the camera or brightening in post gives nearly the same result: the sensor is ISO-invariant.`, r`On many modern sensors, ISO 100 pushed 3 EV in software looks almost the same as ISO 800, but with 3 more stops of highlight headroom.`],
  "Quantisation": [r`Converting a continuous voltage to integers adds a uniform rounding error of standard deviation q/√12. It matters only when the step is comparable to the other noise; otherwise bit depth beyond the noise level adds nothing.`, r`Rounding to whole DN adds 0.29 DN RMS, negligible when read noise is 2 DN.`],
  "Quantum efficiency": [r`The fraction of photons that produce a counted electron depends on wavelength (silicon absorbs blue near the surface and red deep inside), on filters, microlenses and angle. Silicon becomes transparent beyond about 1.1 µm.`, r`Back-illuminated sensors reach 80–95 % in the green; front-illuminated colour sensors 40–60 % including the colour filters.`],
  "Blooming & smear": [r`When a pixel exceeds its full well, charge spills into neighbours; in CCDs it runs along the transfer direction and leaves vertical streaks. CMOS sensors have anti-blooming drains; smear in CCDs also comes from light falling during the transfer.`, r`Saturated stars in astronomical CCD images often show long bleed trails along columns.`],
  "Pixel crosstalk": [r`Light entering a pixel at a steep angle, or charge diffusing sideways, ends up in the neighbouring pixel. In colour sensors this mixes colours (desaturating them towards the corners) and lowers the MTF.`, r`Adapted wide-angle lenses with steep chief rays often show magenta or cyan colour casts at the frame edges.`],
  "Cover-glass aberration": [r`The filter stack in front of a sensor is a plane-parallel plate. It moves the focus back by t(1 − 1/n) and adds spherical aberration that grows with aperture, so lenses designed for one stack thickness lose sharpness on another.`, r`A 2 mm stack with n = 1.5 shifts focus by 0.67 mm; the extra spherical aberration is significant at f/1.4.`],
  "Demosaicing": [r`Each pixel records one colour; the missing two are interpolated from neighbours. Simple bilinear interpolation blurs and creates zipper artefacts; edge-aware and gradient-corrected methods (Malvar–He–Cutler, AHD) are sharper but can mis-guess fine detail.`, r`Raw converters differ mainly in demosaicing and noise reduction, which is why the same raw file can look different.`],
  "Pixel shift & drizzle": [r`Shifting the sensor or the pointing by sub-pixel amounts between exposures samples the image more finely. Combining the frames (drizzle in astronomy, pixel-shift in cameras) recovers full colour per pixel and detail above the native Nyquist limit.`, r`Hubble's undersampled cameras routinely use 4-point dithers and drizzle to halve the effective pixel size.`],
  "Photon transfer curve": [r`For flat-field images, variance against mean signal is a straight line whose slope is the inverse conversion gain and whose intercept is the read noise. Full well appears where the variance collapses. It is the standard way to characterise a sensor.`, r`A slope of 0.5 means 2 e⁻ per DN; an intercept of 4 DN² then means 4 e⁻ of read noise.`],
  "Detective quantum efficiency": [r`DQE measures how much of the information in the incident photons survives, including all detector noise. It is the fairest way to compare detectors: film has a DQE of about 1 %, while modern silicon sensors reach 50–90 %.`, r`A detector with DQE 0.5 needs twice the exposure of an ideal photon counter for the same SNR.`],
  "Dual conversion gain": [r`Switching the pixel's conversion capacitance trades full well for read noise. High gain gives low read noise in the shadows; low gain holds more charge. Cameras switch at a certain ISO, which is why noise can drop suddenly when ISO is raised.`, r`Some cameras switch to the high-gain mode at around ISO 640, so ISO 640 can be cleaner than ISO 500.`],
  "Up-the-ramp sampling": [r`Infrared arrays can be read without being reset. Fitting a line to many reads lowers the effective read noise and allows jumps from cosmic rays to be detected and excised in each pixel.`, r`JWST's NIRCam reads its full frames every 10.7 s; a 1000 s exposure has about 90 reads per pixel.`],
  "Electron-multiplying CCDs": [r`An extra register multiplies the charge by impact ionisation before the amplifier, making read noise negligible. The random gain doubles the variance of the signal (excess-noise factor √2), equivalent to halving the QE, so EMCCDs only pay off at very low signals.`, r`A gain of 1000 makes 50 e⁻ of read noise equivalent to 0.05 e⁻.`],
  "Back-side illumination": [r`In a front-illuminated sensor the light passes the metal wiring first; a back-illuminated sensor is thinned and turned over so light enters from the other side. QE and angular acceptance improve, which made small high-performance pixels possible.`, r`Phone sensors with 1 µm pixels would be impractical without back-side illumination.`],
  "Time-delay integration": [r`In TDI the charge is shifted across the sensor at the speed of the moving image, so each object accumulates signal over many rows without smearing. It is used by Earth-observation satellites and by drift-scanning astronomical surveys.`, r`Gaia scans the sky continuously and reads its CCDs in TDI mode.`],
  // ── Time and motion ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Motion blur": [r`If the image moves during the exposure, each point is smeared along its path. Blur length equals image speed times exposure time; panning matches the camera to the subject to keep it sharp.`, r`A car at 50 km/h 10 m away, through a 50 mm lens at 1/60 s, smears by 1.2 mm on the sensor.`],
  "Shutter angle": [r`Film cameras use a rotating shutter, and its open angle defines the exposure as a fraction of the frame time. The 180° convention gives motion blur that looks natural; smaller angles give a staccato look, larger ones smear.`, r`At 24 fps a 180° shutter exposes for 1/48 s; 45° gives 1/192 s.`],
  "Rolling-shutter distortion": [r`CMOS sensors usually read row by row, so the top and bottom of the frame are captured at different times. Fast motion is sheared, propellers bend and flashes light only part of the frame. Global shutters avoid it.`, r`A propeller at 2400 rpm turns 288° during a 20 ms readout.`],
  "Flicker banding": [r`Lights on AC mains flicker at twice the mains frequency; LEDs may pulse even faster. With a rolling shutter, rows see different parts of the cycle and the image shows bands, unless the exposure is a whole number of flicker periods.`, r`Under 50 Hz mains, exposures of 1/100 s or 1/50 s avoid banding.`],
  "Wagon-wheel effect": [r`A rotating pattern sampled by frames appears to move at its true frequency minus the nearest multiple of the frame rate: wheels slow down, stop or turn backwards.`, r`A 12-spoke wheel turning at 2 rev/s has a spoke frequency of 24 Hz and looks stationary at 24 fps.`],
  "Camera shake": [r`Hand tremor is mostly angular, so the image moves in proportion to the focal length. The reciprocal rule (1/f) is a rough guide; higher-resolution sensors and larger prints demand faster speeds.`, r`A 200 mm lens on an APS-C camera (crop 1.5) needs about 1/300 s by the rule.`],
  "Image stabilisation": [r`Gyros measure the camera's rotation and a moving lens group or sensor shifts the image back. Performance is limited by gyro noise, control-loop bandwidth and translation, which gyros cannot see. Telescopes use fine-guidance sensors and fast steering mirrors.`, r`Modern stabilisers claim 5–8 stops by the CIPA test.`],
  "Pan judder": [r`At 24 fps, a pan that moves the image too far between frames appears to stutter. Cinematographers keep pans slow enough that an object takes several seconds to cross the frame, or add motion blur with a wider shutter.`, r`A common guideline is at least 7 s for an object to cross the frame at 24 fps with a 180° shutter.`],
  "Pointing jitter (space)": [r`Reaction wheels, cryocoolers and thermal snapping shake a space telescope's line of sight. The PSF becomes the optical PSF convolved with the jitter distribution, lowering the Strehl and smearing faint companions.`, r`JWST's line-of-sight jitter is about 1 mas; Hubble's is a few mas.`],
  "Frame-rate conversion & 3:2 pulldown": [r`Showing 24 fps material on a 60 Hz display needs a non-integer ratio, so frames are held alternately for 3 and 2 refreshes. The uneven cadence adds judder to pans; 120 Hz displays avoid it with 5:5.`, r`NTSC video actually runs at 59.94 Hz, so cinema is slowed to 23.976 fps for broadcast.`],
  "Star trails & the 500 rule": [r`Earth rotates by 15″ per second of time, so stars move across the sensor. The "500 rule" (500/focal length in seconds) predates high-resolution sensors and allows trails of several pixels; a per-pixel criterion is much stricter.`, r`With 24 mm and 4 µm pixels the rule allows 21 s, but a star on the celestial equator moves one pixel in 2.3 s.`],
  // ── Atmosphere and environment ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Astronomical seeing": [r`Turbulent cells of different temperature in the atmosphere corrugate the wavefront. A telescope larger than the Fried parameter r₀ sees a seeing disk of width about λ/r₀, independent of aperture; only adaptive optics or space restore λ/D.`, r`r₀ = 10 cm at 500 nm gives 1.0″ seeing, about 60 times the diffraction limit (1.22 λ/D) of an 8 m telescope.`],
  "Kolmogorov turbulence": [r`Energy cascades from large eddies (the outer scale, tens of metres) down to millimetre scales. The resulting refractive-index fluctuations have a power spectrum ∝ κ^−11/3 and a phase structure function 6.88 (r/r₀)^5/3, the basis of all seeing and AO models.`, r`Two points 1 m apart on the pupil differ by about 2.8 waves RMS at 500 nm when r₀ = 10 cm.`],
  "Scintillation (twinkling)": [r`High-altitude turbulence also focuses and defocuses light, so the intensity at the ground fluctuates. Small apertures see strong twinkling; planets twinkle less because their disks average over many independent patterns.`, r`Scintillation noise often limits ground-based photometry of bright stars at the millimagnitude level.`],
  "Adaptive optics": [r`A wavefront sensor measures the turbulence on a guide star and a deformable mirror cancels it hundreds of times per second. The correction is best at long wavelengths and near the guide star.`, r`Keck and the VLT reach Strehl ratios of about 0.5–0.7 in the K band with AO.`],
  "Shack–Hartmann wavefront sensing": [r`A lenslet array divides the pupil into subapertures, each forming a spot whose displacement measures the local wavefront slope. A reconstructor turns slopes into wavefront or mirror commands; noise and spot sampling limit the accuracy.`, r`An 8 m telescope with 40 × 40 lenslets has 20 cm subapertures, about r₀ at 500 nm.`],
  "Greenwood frequency & AO bandwidth": [r`The AO loop must correct faster than the turbulence changes, which is set by wind speed and r₀. The servo-lag error grows as (f<sub>G</sub>/f<sub>3dB</sub>)^5/3, so loops run at 0.5–3 kHz.`, r`Wind of 10 m/s with r₀ = 10 cm gives f<sub>G</sub> = 43 Hz at 500 nm and about 7 Hz in the K band.`],
  "Isoplanatic angle": [r`Light from objects in different directions passes through different turbulence at altitude, so an AO correction is valid only within a small angle. Multi-conjugate AO with several mirrors and guide stars widens it.`, r`With r₀ = 10 cm and turbulence at 5 km, θ₀ ≈ 1.3″ at 500 nm and several times larger in the K band.`],
  "Lucky imaging & speckle interferometry": [r`Exposures shorter than the atmospheric coherence time freeze the turbulence. Selecting the best few per cent (lucky imaging), or combining their Fourier transforms (speckle interferometry), recovers near-diffraction-limited detail without AO.`, r`With r₀ = 10 cm and 10 m/s wind the coherence time is 3 ms.`],
  "Atmospheric refraction": [r`Air bends starlight towards the vertical, so objects appear higher than they are. The effect grows towards the horizon and must be corrected in telescope pointing and astrometry.`, r`At the horizon refraction is about 34′, more than the Sun's diameter: the setting Sun is already geometrically below the horizon.`],
  "Extinction & airmass": [r`Molecules, aerosols and water absorb and scatter starlight; the loss in magnitudes is proportional to the path length through the air (airmass). Photometry corrects it by observing standards at different airmasses.`, r`With k<sub>V</sub> = 0.15 mag per airmass, a star 60° from the zenith (airmass 2) loses 0.3 mag.`],
  "Aerial perspective & haze": [r`Air between camera and scene both attenuates the light from it and adds scattered airlight, so distant objects lose contrast and turn bluish. Landscape painters used it as a depth cue; dehazing algorithms invert it.`, r`A visual range of 20 km corresponds to an extinction coefficient of 0.2 per km (Koschmieder).`],
  "Heat shimmer & mirages": [r`Air near a hot surface is less dense and has a lower index; rays curve away from it and distant objects appear mirrored or wobble. Long telephotos over hot ground are limited by it.`, r`A road mirage shows the sky reflected in an apparently wet patch of asphalt hundreds of metres away.`],
  "Image stacking (√N)": [r`Averaging N exposures reduces random noise by √N while the signal stays constant. Once each sub-exposure is limited by sky noise rather than read noise, splitting the total time into more subs costs almost nothing and allows rejection of satellites and cosmic rays.`, r`Stacking 100 sub-exposures improves the SNR tenfold over one.`],
  "Sky background & light pollution": [r`The night sky glows from airglow, zodiacal light and artificial light scattered by the atmosphere. For faint objects the noise of this background sets the exposure time needed; a brighter sky by Δm magnitudes costs a factor of 10^(0.4Δm) in time.`, r`Moving from a suburban sky (Bortle 6) to a dark site (Bortle 2), with a sky about 2.2 mag darker, cuts the exposure needed for a faint galaxy by a factor of about 7.`],
  "Rayleigh scattering & the blue sky": [r`Molecules much smaller than the wavelength scatter as λ⁻⁴: blue light scatters several times more than red. Skylight is therefore blue, sunsets red as the blue is removed along long paths, and the scattered light polarised.`, r`450 nm light is scattered 5.9 times more than 700 nm light.`],
  "Mie scattering & haze": [r`Particles comparable to or larger than the wavelength scatter strongly forward and almost independently of colour. Haze is white or grey, clouds are white, and the sky near the Sun is bright.`, r`Cloud droplets of about 10 µm scatter all visible wavelengths equally, so clouds are white.`],
  "Fried parameter": [r`r₀ is the diameter over which the turbulent phase varies by about 1 rad RMS. It integrates the turbulence strength along the line of sight and grows as λ^6/5, so seeing improves slowly towards the infrared.`, r`r₀ = 10 cm at 500 nm becomes 59 cm at 2.2 µm.`],
  "Laser guide stars & the cone effect": [r`A laser excites sodium atoms at about 90 km to make an artificial guide star. Its light samples a cone of atmosphere, missing turbulence near the edge of the pupil at altitude; extremely large telescopes need several lasers to fill the cylinder.`, r`The ELT uses six sodium lasers for its tomographic adaptive optics.`],
  "Green flash": [r`Near the horizon, refraction is strong enough that its colour dependence separates the solar limb into stacked coloured images. When the Sun sets the green image disappears last (blue is scattered away), for a second or two, usually enhanced by mirage layers.`, r`The colour separation at the horizon is of order 20″, a small fraction of the Sun's 32′ diameter.`],
  // ── Space telescopes and astronomy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Diffraction-limited space imaging": [r`Above the atmosphere, resolution is set by the aperture alone and the PSF is stable, which allows precise photometry, astrometry and deep imaging of faint galaxies.`, r`JWST at 2 µm resolves 0.077″; Hubble at 500 nm, 0.052″.`],
  "Segment phasing": [r`A segmented mirror acts as one only if all segments sit on the same surface to a small fraction of a wavelength. Coarse phasing uses dispersed fringes, fine phasing uses phase retrieval from defocused images, and edge sensors hold the result on ground-based telescopes.`, r`JWST's 18 segments were aligned to give a total wavefront error near 65 nm RMS, better than its 131 nm requirement.`],
  "Phase retrieval": [r`The phase of the wavefront is not measured directly; it is inferred from intensity images taken with known extra defocus by iteratively fitting a wavefront model. It needs no extra hardware and was used to diagnose Hubble's flaw and to align JWST.`, r`JWST's wavefront sensing uses NIRCam images taken through weak lenses of ±4 and ±8 waves of defocus.`],
  "Coronagraphy": [r`A coronagraph blocks the star's light in the focal plane and then removes its diffracted residue with a stop in a pupil plane (Lyot stop). Advanced designs use phase masks and shaped pupils, and deformable mirrors dig dark holes in the remaining speckles.`, r`Roman's coronagraph instrument aims at contrasts around 10⁻⁹ close to the star.`],
  "Field-dependent PSF": [r`Off-axis aberrations change the PSF across an instrument's field; photometry, astrometry and weak lensing need a PSF model at each position. Tools such as STPSF build it from optical models and measured wavefront maps.`, r`A Hubble-like Ritchey–Chrétien at 600 nm falls from Strehl 1 at the centre to about 0.65 at the corners of a ±4′ field on a curved focal surface.`],
  "Misalignment & nodal aberrations": [r`When the secondary mirror is decentred or tilted, the aberration fields of the two mirrors no longer share a centre. Nodal aberration theory predicts field-constant coma and astigmatism with two zero points; telescope alignment uses these signatures.`, r`A 50 µm decentre of a Hubble-like secondary adds about 130 nm of coma, the same across the whole field.`],
  "Hubble's spherical aberration": [r`Hubble's primary was polished to the wrong conic constant because a spacer in the null corrector was displaced by 1.3 mm. The mirror was perfectly smooth and precisely wrong: 2.2 µm too flat at the edge, giving about half a wave of RMS error at 633 nm.`, r`The 1993 servicing mission installed COSTAR and WFPC2, whose optics carried the opposite spherical aberration.`],
  "Starshades (external occulters)": [r`A starshade tens of metres across, flown tens of thousands of kilometres in front of a telescope, casts a shadow of the star. Its petals are an apodiser that cancels the Poisson spot of a round disk; the telescope itself needs no coronagraph.`, r`A hypergaussian shade with a = b = 12.5 m at 80 000 km suppresses visible starlight to about 10⁻¹¹ at the telescope.`],
  "Speckle floor & wavefront stability": [r`Even tiny sinusoidal ripples of the wavefront scatter light into pairs of speckles that look like planets. A ripple of amplitude h gives speckles of relative intensity about (πh/λ)², so 10⁻¹⁰ contrast demands picometre stability over hours.`, r`A 10 pm ripple at 500 nm gives speckles of 4 × 10⁻⁹, brighter than an Earth.`],
  "Thermal emission & cryogenics": [r`Every warm surface glows in the infrared; at wavelengths beyond about 2 µm the telescope's own emission can swamp faint sources. Infrared observatories are cooled: JWST passively to about 40 K and its mid-infrared instrument to 7 K.`, r`A 300 K mirror radiates most strongly at 9.7 µm, the middle of MIRI's band.`],
  "Undersampling": [r`Nyquist sampling of a diffraction-limited PSF needs pixels of λ/(2D) or smaller. Many space cameras trade sampling for field of view and recover resolution by dithering.`, r`Hubble's WFC3/IR has 0.13″ pixels, about twice λ/(2D) at 1.6 µm.`],
  "Plate scale & astrometry": [r`The plate scale converts millimetres on the detector to arcseconds on the sky. Precise astrometry also needs a model of the field distortion, calibrated on star fields, and corrections for differential aberration of light and refraction.`, r`Hubble's 57.6 m focal length gives 3.58″ per millimetre.`],
  "Sensitivity scaling": [r`For background-limited point sources at the diffraction limit, the signal grows as D² and the background in the shrinking PSF falls as D⁻², so the time to a given SNR falls as D⁻⁴. This is the strongest argument for large apertures.`, r`Doubling the aperture makes such observations 16 times faster.`],
  "Orbital focus breathing": [r`A telescope's structure expands and contracts as it moves in and out of sunlight or changes attitude. The resulting change in mirror spacing defocuses the PSF on time scales of an orbit.`, r`Hubble's secondary-to-primary distance changes by a few micrometres per orbit, visible as PSF changes in precise photometry.`],
  "Charge-transfer inefficiency (CTI)": [r`Radiation damage creates traps in the silicon of CCDs. During readout they capture electrons and release them later, leaving trails behind sources, reducing their flux and shifting their centroids. Pixel-based correction models are essential for weak-lensing shapes.`, r`With ε = 10⁻⁵ per transfer, a source 2000 transfers from the readout loses 2 % of its charge, much more at low signal levels.`],
  "Inter-pixel capacitance (IPC)": [r`In hybrid infrared arrays, capacitive coupling between neighbouring pixels makes a fraction of each signal appear in the neighbours. It blurs the PSF slightly and hides noise in photon-transfer measurements, biasing the measured gain.`, r`With α = 1.5 % coupling to each neighbour, a photon-transfer measurement underestimates the variance by about 11 %.`],
  "Cosmic rays & persistence": [r`Energetic particles deposit charge along tracks through the detector; in space, many pixels are hit in a long exposure. Infrared arrays also retain a latent image of bright sources. Up-the-ramp fitting finds and removes jumps; persistence is modelled and masked.`, r`At L2 about 5 particles per cm² per second hit the detectors: of order a per cent of pixels in a 1000 s exposure.`],
  "Zodiacal light": [r`Sunlight scattered by interplanetary dust, plus its thermal emission in the mid-infrared, is the dominant background for space telescopes. It is brightest towards the ecliptic and the Sun.`, r`At the ecliptic poles the zodiacal light has a surface brightness of about 23 V magnitudes per square arcsecond.`],
  "Gravitational lensing": [r`Mass curves space-time and deflects light, so a galaxy or cluster acts as a lens for sources behind it: arcs, multiple images, Einstein rings and magnification. Weak lensing distorts the shapes of distant galaxies slightly, which maps dark matter.`, r`The Sun deflects starlight grazing its limb by 1.75″, as measured in the 1919 eclipse.`],
  "Einstein radius & mass": [r`A source directly behind a point mass appears as a ring whose angular radius depends on the mass and the distances. For extended lenses the radius measures the mass enclosed within it, independent of its dynamics.`, r`A massive galaxy halfway to a distant source has an Einstein radius of about 1″; a star in the Galactic bulge, about 1 mas.`],
  "Cosmological redshift": [r`Light from distant galaxies is stretched by the expansion of the Universe. Ultraviolet and visible features of the first galaxies arrive in the near- and mid-infrared, which is why JWST observes there.`, r`Lyman-α at 121.6 nm from a galaxy at z = 10 is observed at 1.34 µm.`],
  "Exoplanet contrast": [r`A planet's reflected light relative to its star depends on its albedo, phase and size relative to its orbital radius. In the thermal infrared the contrast is far more favourable but the angular resolution is worse.`, r`The Earth seen from afar is about 10⁻¹⁰ of the Sun in visible light, but about 10⁻⁷ at 10 µm.`],
  "Inner working angle": [r`The inner working angle is the smallest separation at which a coronagraph or starshade delivers its design contrast. It decides which planets in the habitable zones of which stars can be seen.`, r`An Earth at 1 au from a star 10 pc away is 100 mas from it; a 4 m telescope with a 3 λ/D coronagraph at 550 nm reaches 85 mas.`],
  "Pyramid wavefront sensor": [r`A four-sided glass pyramid at the focal plane splits the light into four pupil images; differences between them measure the wavefront slopes. In closed loop it is more sensitive than a Shack–Hartmann sensor, and modulation trades sensitivity for linear range.`, r`Modern extreme-AO systems such as those on the Large Binocular Telescope and Subaru use pyramid sensors.`],
  "Échelle spectrographs": [r`An échelle grating has coarse, steep grooves used in high orders; orders overlap and a prism or grating at right angles (cross-disperser) separates them into a two-dimensional format on the detector.`, r`ESPRESSO reaches a resolving power of 140 000, enough to measure stellar velocities to tens of centimetres per second over time.`],
  "AO error budget": [r`The residual wavefront of an AO system is the quadrature sum of independent terms: fitting error (finite actuators), servo lag, measurement noise, aliasing and anisoplanatism. The Strehl ratio follows from the total by Maréchal.`, r`A total of 100 nm RMS gives S ≈ 0.92 at 2.2 µm but only 0.27 at 550 nm.`],
  "Nulling interferometry": [r`Combining two telescopes with a half-wave phase shift makes their light interfere destructively on axis, nulling the star, while light from a slightly off-axis planet passes through bright fringes.`, r`Bracewell proposed it in 1978; the LIFE mission concept would use it in the mid-infrared to study Earth-like planets.`],
  // ── Lens design and manufacturing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Retrofocus & telephoto designs": [r`A negative group in front of a positive one (retrofocus) moves the rear principal plane back, giving a back focus longer than the focal length for wide-angles on SLRs. The reverse (telephoto) makes a lens shorter than its focal length.`, r`A 14 mm SLR lens needs about 40 mm of back focus to clear the mirror.`],
  "Double-Gauss symmetry": [r`A design symmetric about the stop cancels the odd aberrations (coma, distortion, lateral colour) automatically at unit magnification and nearly at infinity. The double-Gauss is the basis of most standard lenses.`, r`Classic 50 mm f/2 lenses are double-Gauss with six or seven elements.`],
  "Bending & shape factor": [r`A lens of fixed power can be bent into many shapes; the shape factor controls its spherical aberration and coma. For an object at infinity, a nearly plano-convex shape with the curved side facing the object minimises spherical aberration.`, r`For n = 1.5 the minimum lies near q = +0.7; the flat side facing the object instead gives about four times more spherical aberration.`],
  "ED & fluorite elements": [r`Low-dispersion glasses with anomalous partial dispersion, and crystalline calcium fluoride, allow apochromatic correction. They are soft, thermally sensitive and expensive, so they are used sparingly where the chromatic correction matters most.`, r`Long telephotos for sport and wildlife typically contain one to three fluorite or super-ED elements near the front.`],
  "Floating elements": [r`Aberrations of a lens designed for infinity grow at close range. Moving two or more groups by different amounts during focusing keeps the correction, which is essential for macro and fast wide-angle lenses.`, r`Many macro lenses keep their sharpness from infinity to 1:1 only because of floating focus.`],
  "Thermal focus shift": [r`Temperature changes the index of the glasses and the length of the barrel, shifting focus. Long lenses are designed to focus slightly past infinity to allow for it; athermal designs match glass and housing.`, r`A 600 mm telephoto can drift by tens of micrometres over a 30 K swing, outside its depth of focus wide open.`],
  "Anamorphic lenses": [r`Cylindrical elements give different focal lengths horizontally and vertically, squeezing a wide picture onto a narrower sensor area. Oval bokeh, horizontal streak flares and characteristic distortion are the "anamorphic look".`, r`A 2× squeeze on a 4:3 area gives the 2.39:1 cinemascope format after de-squeezing (with some crop).`],
  "Parfocal zooms": [r`A parfocal zoom keeps focus while the focal length changes, so the operator can zoom in to focus and zoom out to frame. It needs carefully coordinated movements of the groups; many stills zooms are only approximately parfocal.`, r`Broadcast and cinema zooms are parfocal across their whole range.`],
  "Tolerancing & yield": [r`Every radius, thickness, index, decentre and tilt has a tolerance. Sensitivity analysis and Monte-Carlo simulation predict the distribution of performance of manufactured lenses, and compensators (focus, spacers) recover some of it.`, r`A sensitive fast prime may need decentres held to about 10 µm to keep 90 % of units within specification.`],
  "Merit function & optimisation": [r`A lens design program defines a merit function, a weighted sum of squared ray errors, MTF targets and constraints, and lowers it by damped least squares or global methods. The designer's skill is in choosing the starting point and the targets.`, r`Optimising a Cooke triplet for a 40° field takes a few hundred iterations of damped least squares.`],
  "Field flatteners": [r`A negative lens close to the image plane has little effect on power or on most aberrations, because the marginal ray height there is small, but it contributes strongly to the Petzval sum and so flattens the field.`, r`Astrographs and fast Schmidt-type cameras use flatteners close to the sensor.`],
  // ── Rendering and perception ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  "Bokeh": [r`The appearance of out-of-focus areas depends on the shape of the aperture, the residual spherical aberration (bright or soft-edged disks), vignetting (cat's eyes), aspheric polishing marks (onion rings) and apodisation. Undercorrected spherical aberration gives smooth background blur.`, r`A lens with residual spherical aberration renders background highlights with soft edges and foreground ones with bright rims.`],
  "Swirly bokeh": [r`Mechanical vignetting turns out-of-focus highlights into cat's eyes oriented tangentially, and field curvature makes the blur grow towards the edges. Together they form a swirl around the centre.`, r`The Helios 44 and Petzval-type portrait lenses are famous for it.`],
  "Micro-contrast": [r`Perceived sharpness and "pop" correlate with MTF at the low and middle spatial frequencies that the eye is most sensitive to at the viewing distance, not with the resolution limit. Metrics such as SQF weight the MTF with the eye's contrast sensitivity.`, r`Two lenses with the same limiting resolution can look very different if one has higher MTF at 10–20 lp/mm.`],
  "The eye as an optical system": [r`The eye has about 60 D of power, mostly from the cornea, an iris from 2 to 8 mm, uncorrected chromatic aberration and a curved retina sampled densely only at the fovea. Its optics and its sampling are well matched at a pupil of about 3 mm.`, r`Foveal cones are spaced about 0.5′ apart, Nyquist-sampling about 60 cycles per degree.`],
  "Stiles–Crawford effect": [r`Cones are waveguides that accept light most efficiently along their axis, so light entering at the pupil edge is less effective. It acts as an apodisation of the eye's pupil that reduces the effect of aberrations.`, r`Light entering 3 mm from the pupil centre is about 35 % as effective as light through the centre.`],
  "Circle of confusion & viewing": [r`The acceptable blur size depends on how the image is viewed: print size, viewing distance and visual acuity. The conventional d/1500 assumes an 8 × 10 inch print viewed at 25 cm by normal vision.`, r`For full frame, d/1500 gives c ≈ 0.029 mm; for large prints viewed close, 0.01 mm is more realistic.`],
  "Colour temperature & white balance": [r`Light sources are described by the temperature of the blackbody (or daylight) of matching colour. Cameras scale the colour channels so that a neutral object renders neutral, which is a simplified chromatic adaptation.`, r`Tungsten light is about 2850 K, noon daylight about 5500 K, overcast sky 6500–7500 K.`],
  "HDR bracketing & exposure fusion": [r`Several exposures cover a wider range than one. A merge weights each pixel by its inverse noise variance to estimate scene radiance, which is then tone-mapped; exposure fusion blends the frames directly using Laplacian pyramids.`, r`Three frames at ±2 EV extend the dynamic range by 4 EV.`],
  "Neutral-density & graduated filters": [r`ND filters cut all light equally for long exposures or wide apertures in daylight; graduated ND filters darken only the sky to balance it with the foreground in one exposure.`, r`An ND 3.0 filter transmits 0.1 %: 10 stops, turning 1/60 s into about 17 s.`],
  "Tone mapping & dynamic range": [r`Scenes can span 20 stops while displays show about 8–10. Tone-mapping operators compress the range, globally (one curve) or locally (preserving local contrast), at the risk of halos and unnatural looks.`, r`Reinhard's global operator L/(1 + L) maps all radiances into [0, 1) with gentle highlight compression.`],
  "Chromatic adaptation": [r`The visual system rescales cone sensitivities to discount the illuminant. Colour science models it with von Kries-type transforms in a sharpened cone space (Bradford, CAT16), which raw converters and colour management use.`, r`A white sheet looks white under both tungsten and daylight, although its spectrum differs strongly.`],
  "Colour rendering (CRI, R9)": [r`The colour rendering index compares how test colours look under a source and under a reference of the same colour temperature. Its general index R<sub>a</sub> averages eight pastel samples, so saturated reds (R9) are reported separately; TM-30 and TLCI are newer measures.`, r`Incandescent lamps have R<sub>a</sub> = 100; cheap LEDs can have R<sub>a</sub> = 80 with R9 below zero.`],
  "Contrast sensitivity function": [r`The eye detects contrast best at a few cycles per degree; sensitivity falls at low frequencies (lateral inhibition) and at high frequencies (optics and cone sampling), reaching zero near 60 c/deg. Image-quality metrics weight the MTF by it.`, r`At 25 cm, a peak of 4 c/deg corresponds to about 1 cycle per millimetre on a print.`],
  "Colour matching functions": [r`The CIE 1931 standard observer gives three functions of wavelength whose integrals with a spectrum predict colour matches. All colorimetry (XYZ, chromaticity, Lab) is built on them; the 1964 10° observer is used for larger fields.`, r`Monochromatic 520 nm light lies near the top of the spectral locus, outside the sRGB gamut.`],
  "Metamerism": [r`Spectra that differ can produce the same tristimulus values, so they match for one observer under one illuminant. Under another light, or for a camera with different spectral sensitivities, the match can break.`, r`Two fabrics that match in the shop can differ in daylight; cameras show "observer metamerism" relative to the eye.`],
  "Colour difference (ΔE₀₀)": [r`CIEDE2000 computes perceived colour differences in a modified CIELAB space with corrections for lightness, chroma, hue and the blue region. It is used to grade camera colour accuracy and print proofs.`, r`ΔE₀₀ ≈ 1 is just noticeable side by side; colour-critical work aims at average ΔE₀₀ below 2.`],
  "Purkinje shift": [r`At low light levels vision switches from cones (peak 555 nm) to rods (peak 507 nm). Reds look darker and blues relatively brighter at dusk; in scotopic vision colour disappears altogether.`, r`Red flowers that are brighter than the leaves at noon look darker than them at dusk.`],
  "Mach bands & acutance": [r`Lateral inhibition in the retina exaggerates the brightness on each side of an edge. Unsharp masking adds similar overshoots, and perceived sharpness (acutance) follows the steepness of edges more than the limiting resolution.`, r`Moderate sharpening with a radius of 1–2 pixels raises acutance visibly without adding detail.`],
};
